n** it completes without a POST_SAVE_FINGERPRINT rejection and the canonical writer has a real non-test call path |\n| REQ-003 | Make PE-gate UPDATE/REINFORCE lanes reachable by dropping the same-path exclusion params from the `findSimilarMemories` call, and fix the tests that mock it to mask the gap (report §3 #26 and Chain E; `pe-orchestration.ts:66-67`, `pe-gating.ts:172-174`) | **Given** an edited doc re-saved at the same path, **When** the PE gate evaluates candidates, **Then** the same-path predecessor is a candidate and the decision is UPDATE or REINFORCE, not CREATE |\n\nCANDIDATES:\n - .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/validation/spec-doc-structure.ts\n===== ROW 47 | basename_only | total: 1\nDOC: specs/sk-design/018-sk-design-parent-v2/001-sk-create-chart/008-evilcharts-reference-research/research/evilcharts-2026-09-03/lineages/deepseek-flash-max/iterations/iteration-003.md LINE: 15\nCIT: ../../../../../../.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-chart/assets/color/palettes.json:1\nCONTEXT:\n**F3-3. The corpus's own system-definition rule says several neutral rows should be categorical.** `color-system.md` defines `categorical` as \"category membership\" for \"unordered categories\" and `neutral` as the fallback \"whenever hue would carry no stable meaning\". A multi-series comparison chart's series identity *is* a category: `grouped-bars` (2 series) is assigned `neutral` while `stacked-bars` (segments) is assigned `categorical` — the same kind of series membership, two different systems. `stacked-area` (2-5 series) is `categorical`, `parallel-axes` is `categorical`, but `grouped-bars` …\n\n…The spec's edge-case list explicitly names \"a choice that only reads well in a dark theme\" and asks whether the corpus should gain one. [SOURCE: context/evilcharts/src/app/globals.css:102-126] [SOURCE: ../../../../../../.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-chart/references/color-system.md:75] [SOURCE: ../../../../../../.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-chart/assets/color/palettes.json:1]…\n\n**F3-5. Numeric text is set in a mono face with tabular figures in evilcharts; the corpus uses the system face.** Tooltip values are `font-mono font-medium tabular-nums` (JetBrains Mono on the site, `font-mono` token). The corpus applies `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` to tick labels only (daily-line `.tick`); value labels inside the figure, notes and the source line do not. The exact fonts are not adoptable (the contract bans web fonts), but the *treatment* is: a system mono stack (`ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace`) costs nothing and needs no network. [SOURCE: context/evil…\nCANDIDATES:\n - .skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/assets/style-reference/evilcharts/palettes.json\n===== ROW 48 | basename_only | total: 1\nDOC: specs/system-speckit/026-graph-and-context-optimization/000-release-and-program-cleanup/018-program-research-and-remediation/research/iterations/D2.md LINE: 39\nCIT: changelog-002-013-post-deprecation-deep-review.md:27\nCONTEXT:\n### Angle 24: CocoIndex decoupling — capability loss and re-addition value\n- **Framing:** Decoupling CocoIndex from the code-graph lost specific capabilities (Python-based indexing, cross-encoder reranking, semantic search) that are either replaced, abandoned, or worth re-adding.\n- **Repo evidence:** `changelog-002-001-touchpoint-research.md:29` — \"D1 accepts the loss of opt-in cross-encoder reranking in memory (default path unaffected, positional fallback). D2 adopts a HYBRID search policy (Grep plus code-graph structural) as the post-coco semantic-search replacement.\" `changelog-002-013-post-deprecation-deep-review.md:27` — post-deprecation review found 7 residual items (1 P0: GEMINI.md routing to deleted `mcp__cocoindex_code__search`; 2 P1: memory:manage still declares removed `ccc` subcommands). `changelog-002-deprecate-coco-index-root.md` — 7 child phases from tou…\n- **Findings:** Three capability classes were lost: (1) **Cross-encoder reranking** — opt-in in memory, positional fallback exists, explicitly accepted as a loss in D1. No measured retrieval-precision impact documented. (2) **Python-based incremental indexing** — CocoIndex's Rust/Python indexer had memoization, stable-path keys, and component-level cache-hit guards. These ideas are being re-imported into spec-kit-memory via 027 packets (tombstones, statediff, memoization) — so the capability isn't lost, it's being rebuilt in TypeScript. (3) **Semantic search** — replaced by HYBRID policy (Grep…\n- **Next experiment:** Run the same 20-query retrieval benchmark against (a) the current HYBRID search, (b) a hypothetical code-graph + cosine-similarity hybrid. Measure recall@10 delta.\nCANDIDATES:\n - specs/system-speckit/026-graph-and-context-optimization/changelog/004-code-graph/changelog-002-013-post-deprecation-deep-review.md\n===== ROW 49 | basename_only | total: 1\nDOC: specs/cli-orca/002-consolidate-official-orca-skills/research/research.md LINE: 42\nCIT: skill-guides/computer-use.md:14\nCONTEXT:\n### F4. Boundaries are explicit in the upstream guides\n\nThe upstream guides state their own exclusions. The computer-use guide excludes the embedded browser and points at `orca-cli`. The `orca-cli` guide keeps browser work inside its own `browser` subcommand family rather than sending it to a generic browser tool, and keeps supervised multi-agent work with `orchestration`. [SOURCE: `skill-guides/computer-use.md:14`; `skill-guides/orca-cli.md:33-45`]\n\n### F5. Handoff creation has a documented default shape\nCANDIDATES:\n - .skilled/skills/cli-orca/references/orca-skills/computer-use.md\n===== ROW 50 | basename_only | total: 1\nDOC: specs/system-speckit/033-system-speckit-v4/021-decommission-debt-and-cli-nesting/002-scripts-into-runtime-nesting/review/lineages/luna-max-review-6/iterations/iteration-005.md LINE: 39\nCIT: dist-freshness.cjs:33\nCONTEXT:\n\n## Integration Evidence\n- `dist-freshness.cjs:33-53` has a current `system-spec-kit/runtime/cli` entry with defaul