ode-quality/SKILL.md:106] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-code/code-quality/SKILL.md:119] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec_folder_authoring_checklist.md:22] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills…\n3. Advisor integration should be a parent-layer backlog item, not a packet-local metadata change. The parent registry says the advisor routes the single `sk-code` identity and the hub resolves workflow mode; the quality mode already has aliases, graph metadata already includes `code-quality`, `quality-gate`, `alignment verifier`, and `stable jsonl keys`, while the hub router lacks several `code-quality` activation phrases such as `authoring checklist`, `OpenCode checklist`, `MCP server authoring`, `spec folder authoring`, and `check before done`. The backlog should update parent metadata/route…\n4. Behavior-benchmark work should add two scenarios after the handoff schema exists: a comment-hygiene hook branch scenario and a deep-review consumption scenario. The current playbook has `TH-001` for dist-staleness hook wiring and explicitly states that the comment-hygiene hook branch lacks both automated and manual coverage; meanwhile the hook itself warns on `Write|Edit`, runs the comment-hygiene checker, prints warning text, may also print dist-staleness output, and always exits 0. A realistic scenario should validate warn-only hook output, direct checker confirmation/fix through `code-qu…\nCANDIDATES:\n - .hermes/skills/agent-ai-council/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/agent-code/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/agent-context/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/agent-debug/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/agent-deep-improvement/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/agent-deep-research/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/agent-deep-review/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/agent-design/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/agent-markdown/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/agent-orchestrate/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/agent-prompt-improver/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/agent-review/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/cli-classifier/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/cli-claude-code/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/cli-codex/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/cli-cursor/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/cli-devin/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/cli-external-orchestration/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/cli-hermes/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/cli-jev/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/cli-opencode/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/cli-orca/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/cli-pi/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/deep-ai-council/SKILL.md\n - .hermes/skills/deep-improvement/SKILL.md\n===== ROW 129 | basename_only | total: 1\nDOC: specs/sk-code/001-sk-code-parent/025-code-quality-and-shared-research/research/iterations/iteration-004.md LINE: 14\nCIT: .opencode/skills/sk-code/manual_testing_playbook/manual_testing_playbook.md:292\nCONTEXT:\n2. The highest-value deep-review handoff is traceable quality evidence: deep-review produces a verdict, findings registry, dashboard, iteration audit trail, and review report, while its binary gates require concrete evidence, state integrity, and integration naming. `code-quality` can make that cheaper by handing over modified files, checklist paths, comment-hygiene/dist-staleness results, P0/P1/P2 decisions, accepted deferrals, and exact integration names; deep-review can then audit traceability/maintainability without re-discovering author-side gate state. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/deep-loop…\n3. Hook signals are useful early warnings but not sufficient quality-pass evidence. `code-quality` documents three comment-hygiene enforcement layers: write-time warning, pre-commit block, and CI block; the PostToolUse hook runs only on `Write|Edit`, invokes comment-hygiene and dist-staleness checkers, prints warnings, and always exits 0. The quality gate should therefore treat hook output as a triage source, then require direct checker/quality evidence before handoff or completion-adjacent claims. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-code/code-quality/SKILL.md:124] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-code/c…\n…o. The design-restraint scenario `DR-003` separately validates a durable `ceiling:` comment that must pass hygiene without allow-listing. A useful next benchmark slice is: hook warning appears -> `code-quality` direct checker confirms/fixes -> handoff record feeds deep-review traceability. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-code/manual_testing_playbook/manual_testing_playbook.md:292] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-code/manual_testing_playbook/manual_testing_playbook.md:298] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-code/manual_testing_playbook/manual_testing_playbook.md:322] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-code/manua…\n5. General software-quality standards already align across author, coder, and review surfaces, but the handoff shape is implicit. `code-quality` success criteria require checklist loading, per-file comment-hygiene results, P0/P1/P2 handling, and verification handoff; universal standards define the shared P0/P1/P2 completion impact; the coder acceptance rubric repeats quality-gate and verification evidence as completion blockers. The improvement is to make the existing alignment explicit as durable handoff fields rather than duplicating standards text in every consumer. [SOURCE: .opencode/skill…\n\nCANDIDATES:\n - specs/sk-doc/z_archive/016-create-diff-mode/013-catalog-playbook-snippet-denumbering/002-migration-tooling-and-dry-run/tooling/fixtures/collide/manual_testing_playbook/manual_testing_playbook.md\n===== ROW 130 | ambiguous | total: 464\nDOC: specs/system-skill-advisor/025-mcp-decommission-cli-front-door/011-deep-research-swe-2/research/lineages/swe-2-research/iterations/iteration-005.md LINE: 31\nCIT: goal.md:143\nCONTEXT:\n\n- **Assertion residue at larger scale**: \"214 hits in 115 live files that still presented the memory database, daemon, server, tools or retired commands as existing were swept by area\" — after its token sweep already read zero. Same class C1, same miss-then-claims-hunt shape. [SOURCE: file:specs/system-speckit/033-system-speckit-v4/017-memory-database-decommission/goal.md:1